<!-- ============================================================
     UAC-0145 Sandworm: EtherHiding / GHETTOVIBE / SCOUTCURL
     Threat: UAC-0145 (Sandworm / GRU Unit 74455) ClickFix fake
             CAPTCHA campaign targeting Ukrainian entities.
             Compromised Ukrainian websites inject SMARTAXE cloaking
             JS which, on human visit, copies a PowerShell command to
             the clipboard and instructs Win+R paste.
     Attack chain:
       1. ClickFix Win+R PS one-liner (T1204.003, T1059.001)
       2. GHETTOVIBE VBS dropped to Startup folder (T1547.001)
       3. SCOUTCURL PS recon on each logon (T1059.001, T1082, T1016)
       4. FluidLeech / LoadLoop loaders downloaded by GHETTOVIBE
       5. EtherHiding C2: eth_call to public Ethereum RPC endpoint
          resolves C2 domain from actor smart contract storage slot
          (T1102.002) - C2 cannot be sinkholed via DNS seizure
     Source: CERT-UA advisory #6318437 (2026-07-17)
     MITRE ATT&amp;CK Techniques:
       T1102.002 - Web Service: Bidirectional Communication (EtherHiding)
       T1547.001 - Boot or Logon Autostart: Startup Folder (GHETTOVIBE)
       T1059.001 - PowerShell (SCOUTCURL + ClickFix delivery)
       T1059.005 - Visual Basic (GHETTOVIBE VBS)
       T1204.003 - User Execution: Malicious Image (ClickFix Win+R)
       T1082     - System Information Discovery (SCOUTCURL)
       T1016     - System Network Configuration Discovery (SCOUTCURL)
       T1057     - Process Discovery (SCOUTCURL)
     Rule IDs: 104044-104053
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team)
     Date: 2026-07-20

     Distinction from EtherRAT (rules 104014-104019):
       EtherRAT uses a Teams vishing delivery chain with a Node.js RAT
       and Ethereum C2. UAC-0145 uses a ClickFix VBS/PowerShell chain.
       Rule 104048 (EtherHiding network indicator) applies to both
       campaigns; correlate with process chain rules 104044-104052 to
       distinguish UAC-0145 from EtherRAT by parent process ancestry.

     Log source prerequisites:
       Rule 104044: Sysmon EventID 11 (FileCreate) - Wazuh SID 61644.
         Sysmon must log .vbs file creation in AppData paths; configure
         TargetFilename include filters to cover Startup folder paths.
       Rules 104045-104047, 104049-104052: Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate)
         - Wazuh SID 61603. Sysmon must capture CommandLine and ParentImage
         fields (default in most sysmon configs).
       Rules 104048, 104053: Network / firewall / proxy logs forwarded
         to Wazuh SID 103000 group. Destination hostname must appear in
         the raw log. FortiGate, pfSense, Suricata, Sysmon EventID 3, or
         HTTP proxy logs are all suitable sources.

     Severity tiers:
       12 - ClickFix Win+R spawn (social engineering context, moderate)
       13 - wscript.exe spawning PowerShell (GHETTOVIBE-to-SCOUTCURL)
       13 - SCOUTCURL recon cmdlets from VBS parent (discovery confirmed)
       13 - wscript.exe executing VBS from Startup (GHETTOVIBE active)
       14 - VBS written to Startup folder by PowerShell (GHETTOVIBE drop)
       14 - Ethereum RPC connection (EtherHiding C2 network indicator)
       14 - PowerShell cmdLine referencing Ethereum RPC domain
       14 - eth_call or eth_getStorageAt in commandLine
       15 - Sustained Ethereum RPC beaconing (active EtherHiding confirmed)

     References:
       CERT-UA advisory #6318437 (2026-07-17):
         https://cert.gov.ua/article/6318437
       GuardioLabs EtherHiding technique (2023):
         https://labs.guard.io/etherhiding-hiding-web2-malicious-code-in-web3-smart-contracts-65ea78efad16
       MITRE ATT&amp;CK - Sandworm Team (G0034):
         https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0034/
       MITRE ATT&amp;CK - T1102.002 Web Service Bidirectional Communication:
         https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1102/002/
       MITRE ATT&amp;CK - T1547.001 Startup Folder:
         https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1547/001/
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="threat_intel,command_and_control,persistence,execution,initial_access,discovery,windows,sandworm,uac0145,etherhiding,ghettovibe,scoutcurl,clickfix,">

  <!-- ===================================================
       Rule 104044: GHETTOVIBE - VBS dropped to Startup folder by PowerShell
       Sysmon EventID 11 (FileCreate) - Wazuh SID 61644
       MITRE: T1547.001 (Startup Folder), T1059.001 (PowerShell)

       GHETTOVIBE is deposited in the Windows Startup folder by the
       initial ClickFix Win+R PowerShell one-liner. Files in the Startup
       folder execute automatically at user logon without any registry
       modification, bypassing registry-based persistence detections.
       PowerShell, cmd, or mshta creating a VBS file under the user or
       system Startup folder is a reliable GHETTOVIBE delivery indicator.

       Startup folder paths:
         user:   %APPDATA%\Microsoft\Windows\Start Menu\Programs\Startup
         system: %ProgramData%\Microsoft\Windows\Start Menu\Programs\StartUp

       False positives: extremely rare. No legitimate enterprise
       application deploys VBS scripts to the Startup folder via
       PowerShell. Group Policy logon scripts use dedicated paths and
       are not created by interactive PowerShell processes.
       =================================================== -->
  <rule id="104044" level="14">
    <if_sid>61644</if_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^11$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(?:powershell|pwsh|cmd|mshta)\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetFilename" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(?:AppData\\Roaming|ProgramData)\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Start\s+Menu\\Programs\\Startup\\[^\\]+\.vb[se]$</field>
    <description>UAC-0145/GHETTOVIBE: $(win.eventdata.image) created VBS file in Windows Startup folder on $(win.eventdata.computer) by $(win.eventdata.user) [file: $(win.eventdata.targetFilename)]  -  GHETTOVIBE VBS dropper deposited by ClickFix Win+R PowerShell one-liner; VBS in Startup folder auto-executes at every user logon without registry modification; check browser history for compromised Ukrainian sites (SMARTAXE ClickFix lure), investigate for SCOUTCURL recon output, and check network logs for EtherHiding C2 connections to Ethereum RPC endpoints (T1547.001, T1059.001, CERT-UA #6318437 UAC-0145/Sandworm)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1547.001</id>
      <id>T1059.001</id>
      <id>T1059.005</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sandworm,uac0145,ghettovibe,clickfix,startup_persistence,vbs_dropper,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===================================================
       Rule 104045: GHETTOVIBE - wscript.exe executing VBS from Startup folder
       Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603
       MITRE: T1547.001 (Startup Folder), T1059.005 (Visual Basic)

       After GHETTOVIBE is dropped to the Startup folder, Windows
       auto-executes it at logon via wscript.exe. When wscript.exe
       commandLine references a path under the user or system Startup
       folder, it confirms GHETTOVIBE is active and persisting on
       the host. This fires on every logon until the VBS is removed.

       Distinguish from legitimate logon VBS scripts by checking the
       full Startup folder path and the wscript.exe parent (should be
       the user shell, userinit.exe, or logon process, not an attacker
       pivot). Correlate with rule 104044 to confirm the same VBS was
       previously written by a suspicious process.

       False positives: extremely rare. Enterprises rarely deploy VBS
       scripts via the user Startup folder. Investigate before tuning.
       =================================================== -->
  <rule id="104045" level="13">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\w?script\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(?:AppData\\Roaming|ProgramData)\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Start\s+Menu\\Programs\\Startup\\[^\\]+\.vb[se]</field>
    <description>UAC-0145/GHETTOVIBE: wscript.exe executing VBS from Windows Startup folder on $(win.eventdata.computer) by $(win.eventdata.user) [$(win.eventdata.commandLine)]  -  GHETTOVIBE VBS persistence is auto-executing at logon; fires on every logon while the dropper remains in the Startup folder; remove the VBS file, investigate for EtherHiding C2 connections (rule 104048) and SCOUTCURL recon output, and trace the initial ClickFix lure in browser history (T1547.001, T1059.005, UAC-0145/Sandworm)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1547.001</id>
      <id>T1059.005</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sandworm,uac0145,ghettovibe,startup_persistence,vbs_execution,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===================================================
       Rule 104046: GHETTOVIBE-to-SCOUTCURL chain - wscript.exe spawning PS
       Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603
       MITRE: T1059.001 (PowerShell), T1059.005 (Visual Basic)

       GHETTOVIBE VBS invokes SCOUTCURL as a child PowerShell process.
       The parent-child relationship wscript.exe to powershell.exe is the
       primary behavioral indicator of the GHETTOVIBE-to-SCOUTCURL
       handoff and fires regardless of whether rule 104044 / 104045 was
       triggered (if the VBS file events were missed, this is the next
       detection opportunity in the attack chain).

       Enterprise policy does not typically allow VBS scripts to spawn
       PowerShell directly. When wscript.exe or cscript.exe is the
       direct parent of powershell.exe, this anomalous script execution
       chain warrants investigation in all environments.

       Correlate with rules 104044/104045 on the same host to confirm
       the GHETTOVIBE-SCOUTCURL chain specifically.

       False positives: legacy enterprise automation using VBS to invoke
       PowerShell. Establish a baseline and allowlist known-good paths.
       =================================================== -->
  <rule id="104046" level="13">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.parentImage" type="pcre2">(?i)\\w?script\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(?:powershell|pwsh)\.exe$</field>
    <description>UAC-0145/GHETTOVIBE-SCOUTCURL: wscript.exe spawned PowerShell on $(win.eventdata.computer) by $(win.eventdata.user) [cmd: $(win.eventdata.commandLine)]  -  GHETTOVIBE VBS invokes SCOUTCURL PowerShell recon module as a child process; wscript spawning powershell is the GHETTOVIBE-to-SCOUTCURL handoff signature; correlate with rules 104044/104045 for Startup VBS context and rule 104048 for EtherHiding Ethereum RPC network activity (T1059.001, T1059.005, UAC-0145/Sandworm)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1059.001</id>
      <id>T1059.005</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sandworm,uac0145,ghettovibe,scoutcurl,wscript_spawning_powershell,execution_chain,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===================================================
       Rule 104047: SCOUTCURL - PowerShell recon commands from wscript parent
       Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603
       MITRE: T1059.001, T1082 (SysInfo Discovery), T1016 (Net Config)

       SCOUTCURL is a PowerShell recon module that collects hostname, OS
       version, installed software, running processes, network config,
       domain membership, and user/group accounts, then transmits results
       to C2 (compressed and base64-encoded) via Invoke-WebRequest or
       curl. This rule matches PowerShell spawned by wscript.exe with
       system discovery or web exfiltration commands in the commandLine.

       Covered SCOUTCURL behaviors:
         - System enumeration: whoami, systeminfo, Get-WmiObject,
           Get-CimInstance, tasklist, Get-Process, Get-Service
         - Network enumeration: ipconfig, net user, net group,
           Get-NetAdapter, Get-NetIPAddress
         - Data exfiltration: Invoke-WebRequest, DownloadString,
           DownloadFile, curl/wget to HTTP endpoints

       Correlate with rule 104046 on the same host; that rule fires on
       the wscript-to-PowerShell spawn without commandLine filtering
       while this rule provides richer SCOUTCURL command confirmation.

       False positives: IT admin scripts invoking PowerShell from VBS
       with discovery commands. The wscript.exe parent constraint limits
       false positives significantly versus raw PowerShell recon rules.
       =================================================== -->
  <rule id="104047" level="13">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.parentImage" type="pcre2">(?i)\\w?script\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(?:powershell|pwsh)\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:whoami|net\s+(?:user|group|localgroup)|ipconfig|tasklist|systeminfo|Get-(?:WmiObject|CimInstance|Process|Service|NetAdapter|NetIPAddress)|Invoke-WebRequest|DownloadString|DownloadFile|iwr\s+http|curl\s+http|wget\s+http)</field>
    <description>UAC-0145/SCOUTCURL: PowerShell spawned by wscript.exe running system recon or exfiltration commands on $(win.eventdata.computer) by $(win.eventdata.user) [cmd: $(win.eventdata.commandLine)]  -  SCOUTCURL PowerShell module collects hostname, OS, processes, network config, users, and domain membership then transmits compressed base64-encoded output to C2 via Invoke-WebRequest; wscript.exe parent confirms GHETTOVIBE delivery chain; check Startup folder for VBS (rule 104044) and network logs for EtherHiding Ethereum RPC connections (rule 104048) (T1059.001, T1082, T1016, T1057, UAC-0145/Sandworm)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1059.001</id>
      <id>T1082</id>
      <id>T1016</id>
      <id>T1057</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sandworm,uac0145,scoutcurl,powershell_recon,discovery,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===================================================
       Rule 104048: EtherHiding - Ethereum JSON-RPC endpoint connection
       Network / firewall / proxy logs - Wazuh SID 103000
       MITRE: T1102.002 (Web Service: Bidirectional Communication)

       EtherHiding resolves C2 by calling eth_call against an actor-
       controlled Ethereum smart contract using a public JSON-RPC
       endpoint. The C2 domain lives in blockchain state, so defenders
       cannot sinkhole it by seizing the domain; the actor updates the
       contract storage to a new domain instantly. UAC-0145 FluidLeech
       and LoadLoop use this to resolve their C2 on each execution.

       EtherRAT (Teams vishing campaign, rules 104014-104019) also uses
       the same public Ethereum RPC endpoints. This rule is not exclusive
       to UAC-0145. Correlate with rules 104044-104047 (GHETTOVIBE VBS,
       SCOUTCURL chain) to attribute to UAC-0145 versus EtherRAT
       (wscript parent chain vs. Teams vishing parent chain).

       Any connection from a corporate endpoint to a public Ethereum
       JSON-RPC provider is anomalous. Web3 DApp developers use browser
       extensions or dedicated DApp clients, not enterprise script
       interpreters like wscript.exe or powershell.exe.

       False positives: blockchain developer workstations, Ethereum node
       operator tooling, crypto portfolio apps. Scope with asset group
       filters to exclude known Ethereum developer machines.
       =================================================== -->
  <rule id="104048" level="14">
    <if_sid>103000</if_sid>
    <match>cloudflare-eth\.com|mainnet\.infura\.io|eth-mainnet\.g\.alchemy\.com|rpc\.ankr\.com|ethereum\.publicnode\.com|rpc\.flashbots\.net|eth\.llamarpc\.com|rpc\.mevblocker\.io|api\.mycryptoapi\.com|gateway\.tenderly\.co|eth-mainnet\.public\.blastapi\.io|1rpc\.io|endpoints\.omniatech\.io</match>
    <description>EtherHiding/UAC-0145: Connection to public Ethereum JSON-RPC endpoint on $(agent.name)  -  EtherHiding technique used by UAC-0145 FluidLeech/LoadLoop (CERT-UA #6318437) and EtherRAT (Teams vishing, rules 104014-104019): malware queries public Ethereum RPC to retrieve the current C2 domain from an actor smart contract via eth_call; C2 cannot be sinkholed since the address lives in blockchain state; no legitimate enterprise script or automation tool queries Ethereum JSON-RPC; correlate with process ancestry rules 104044-104047 for GHETTOVIBE chain or rules 104014-104016 for EtherRAT chain (T1102.002, T1008)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1102.002</id>
      <id>T1008</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sandworm,uac0145,etherhiding,ethereum_rpc,blockchain_c2,command_and_control,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===================================================
       Rule 104049: EtherHiding - PowerShell commandLine contains Ethereum RPC
       Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603
       MITRE: T1102.002 (Web Service: Bidirectional Communication), T1059.001

       SCOUTCURL constructs the Ethereum RPC request inline and passes
       the endpoint URL as a commandLine argument to Invoke-WebRequest
       or curl. When a known public Ethereum JSON-RPC provider hostname
       appears directly in the PowerShell commandLine, this confirms the
       EtherHiding C2 resolution call is embedded in the script body or
       passed as an argument string visible to Sysmon.

       This rule complements rule 104048 (network log) by tying the
       Ethereum RPC network connection to a specific PowerShell process.
       Both rules firing on the same host within minutes confirms active
       EtherHiding C2 resolution via SCOUTCURL.

       False positives: blockchain developer scripts calling Ethereum
       RPC from PowerShell. Unusual on non-developer endpoints.
       =================================================== -->
  <rule id="104049" level="14">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(?:powershell|pwsh|curl)\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:cloudflare-eth\.com|mainnet\.infura\.io|eth-mainnet\.g\.alchemy\.com|rpc\.ankr\.com|ethereum\.publicnode\.com|rpc\.flashbots\.net|eth\.llamarpc\.com|rpc\.mevblocker\.io|api\.mycryptoapi\.com|gateway\.tenderly\.co|eth-mainnet\.public\.blastapi\.io|1rpc\.io|endpoints\.omniatech\.io)</field>
    <description>EtherHiding/UAC-0145/SCOUTCURL: $(win.eventdata.image) commandLine references Ethereum JSON-RPC endpoint on $(win.eventdata.computer) by $(win.eventdata.user) [cmd: $(win.eventdata.commandLine)]  -  SCOUTCURL invokes Invoke-WebRequest or curl with an Ethereum RPC URL to perform eth_call C2 domain resolution; PowerShell with Ethereum RPC domain in commandLine is a high-confidence EtherHiding execution indicator; correlate with wscript parent (rule 104046) and network connection (rule 104048) on the same host to confirm UAC-0145 GHETTOVIBE chain (T1102.002, T1059.001, UAC-0145/Sandworm)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1102.002</id>
      <id>T1059.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sandworm,uac0145,etherhiding,scoutcurl,ethereum_rpc,powershell_c2,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===================================================
       Rule 104050: EtherHiding - eth_call or eth_getStorageAt in commandLine
       Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603
       MITRE: T1102.002 (Web Service: Bidirectional Communication)

       EtherHiding uses specific Ethereum JSON-RPC methods:
         eth_call - executes a call to a contract function without a
           transaction; used to invoke the contract getter that returns
           the current C2 domain from the storage slot
         eth_getStorageAt - directly reads a storage slot by index; used
           to retrieve the raw C2 value from the actor contract
         eth_getLogs - used in some EtherHiding variants to retrieve C2
           data from emitted contract events rather than storage reads

       When PowerShell, curl, or a script engine constructs the JSON-RPC
       request body inline, the method name appears in the Sysmon
       commandLine. These method names have virtually no legitimate use
       outside of Ethereum developer tooling running in script engines.

       This rule covers any script interpreter calling these methods,
       not just SCOUTCURL; it therefore also fires for EtherRAT Node.js
       variants if the method appears in a spawned shell commandLine.

       False positives: Ethereum developer tools testing JSON-RPC calls
       from PowerShell scripts; rare on enterprise endpoints.
       =================================================== -->
  <rule id="104050" level="14">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)\beth_(?:call|getLogs|getStorageAt|getBalance|blockNumber)\b</field>
    <description>EtherHiding/UAC-0145: Ethereum JSON-RPC method detected in process commandLine on $(win.eventdata.computer) by $(win.eventdata.user) [process: $(win.eventdata.image), cmd: $(win.eventdata.commandLine)]  -  EtherHiding C2 uses eth_call or eth_getStorageAt to query a smart contract slot for the current C2 domain; JSON-RPC method name in a script interpreter commandLine confirms EtherHiding blockchain C2 resolution logic is executing; extremely rare in legitimate use outside Ethereum developer tooling (T1102.002, UAC-0145/Sandworm CERT-UA #6318437)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1102.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sandworm,uac0145,etherhiding,eth_call,blockchain_c2,command_and_control,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===================================================
       Rule 104051: ClickFix - explorer.exe spawning PowerShell download cradle
       Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603
       MITRE: T1204.003 (User Execution: Malicious Image), T1059.001

       The UAC-0145 ClickFix lure instructs the victim to press Win+R,
       paste a PowerShell command (auto-copied by SMARTAXE JS on the
       compromised site), and press Enter. Win+R executes processes
       through the Windows Explorer shell, making explorer.exe the
       parent of the spawned PowerShell process.

       Detecting explorer.exe spawning PowerShell with a download cradle
       (DownloadString, Invoke-WebRequest, iwr, curl, New-Object
       Net.WebClient) identifies the ClickFix Win+R delivery step and
       is the earliest Sysmon-detectable event in the UAC-0145 chain.

       This rule fires before GHETTOVIBE reaches disk (rule 104044),
       giving defenders a pre-persistence detection opportunity. If this
       fires, check for a VBS file subsequently created in the Startup
       folder on the same host.

       False positives: admins running PowerShell download commands via
       Win+R. The download cradle commandLine pattern distinguishes
       malicious from routine Win+R powershell.exe invocations.
       =================================================== -->
  <rule id="104051" level="12">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.parentImage" type="pcre2">(?i)\\explorer\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(?:powershell|pwsh)\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:DownloadString|DownloadFile|Invoke-WebRequest|iwr\s+http|Start-BitsTransfer|New-Object\s+Net\.WebClient|\.Download(?:String|File)\s*\(|curl\s+http|wget\s+http)</field>
    <description>UAC-0145/ClickFix: explorer.exe spawned PowerShell with download cradle on $(win.eventdata.computer) by $(win.eventdata.user) [cmd: $(win.eventdata.commandLine)]  -  UAC-0145 ClickFix CAPTCHA lure instructs victim to paste a clipboard PowerShell command via Win+R; Win+R execution appears as explorer.exe spawning powershell.exe; download cradle in commandLine confirms malicious clipboard delivery rather than routine admin use; earliest detection point in UAC-0145 chain; check Startup folder for GHETTOVIBE VBS within minutes of this alert (T1204.003, T1059.001, UAC-0145/Sandworm)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1204.003</id>
      <id>T1059.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sandworm,uac0145,clickfix,winr_powershell,download_cradle,initial_access,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===================================================
       Rule 104052: ClickFix - explorer.exe spawning VBS script engine
       Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603
       MITRE: T1204.003 (User Execution: Malicious Image), T1059.005

       In UAC-0145 ClickFix variants where the clipboard command
       directly invokes a VBS or MSHTA payload rather than a PowerShell
       download cradle, explorer.exe spawns the VBS engine (wscript.exe,
       cscript.exe) or mshta.exe directly from the Win+R Run dialog.

       This rule covers direct VBS ClickFix delivery as an alternative
       to rule 104051 (PowerShell download cradle). Both cover Win+R
       entry points with different payload types. Together they provide
       full coverage regardless of which script engine the ClickFix
       clipboard command invokes.

       False positives: users running mshta.exe or wscript.exe from the
       Run dialog for administrative purposes. Uncommon in typical
       enterprise environments. Examine the commandLine for suspicious
       URLs, encoded content, or temp-path scripts.
       =================================================== -->
  <rule id="104052" level="12">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.parentImage" type="pcre2">(?i)\\explorer\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(?:mshta|wscript|cscript)\.exe$</field>
    <description>UAC-0145/ClickFix: explorer.exe spawned VBS script engine $(win.eventdata.image) on $(win.eventdata.computer) by $(win.eventdata.user) [cmd: $(win.eventdata.commandLine)]  -  UAC-0145 ClickFix variants may deliver VBS or MSHTA payloads directly via Win+R clipboard paste; explorer spawning mshta.exe, wscript.exe, or cscript.exe from Run dialog is the Win+R entry point for direct VBS-stage ClickFix delivery; check commandLine for URLs or encoded content and correlate with Startup VBS rules 104044/104045 on this host (T1204.003, T1059.005, UAC-0145/Sandworm)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1204.003</id>
      <id>T1059.005</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sandworm,uac0145,clickfix,winr_vbs,initial_access,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===================================================
       Rule 104053: EtherHiding beaconing - sustained Ethereum RPC connections
       Frequency rule on 104048 - same source IP, 3 hits in 180 seconds
       MITRE: T1102.002 (Web Service: Bidirectional Communication)

       A single Ethereum RPC connection (rule 104048) could be a
       one-shot EtherHiding C2 resolution or a false positive from a
       blockchain developer tool. Three or more connections from the
       same source within 180 seconds indicates the implant is performing
       repeated C2 domain lookups consistent with FluidLeech / LoadLoop
       retrying on startup or polling for C2 domain updates after the
       actor rotates the smart contract storage slot.

       This pattern confirms active EtherHiding C2 communication rather
       than a single resolution attempt. When this fires, treat the host
       as actively compromised.

       Incident response steps:
         - Isolate host from network immediately
         - Capture volatile memory before shutdown
         - Identify and remove GHETTOVIBE VBS from Startup folder
         - Search for FluidLeech / LoadLoop in %TEMP% and %APPDATA%
         - Review browser history for ClickFix lure sites
         - Notify Security Operations Manager and file incident report
       =================================================== -->
